
RTK Query Cache Explained
RTK Query is the data fetching and caching tool included in Redux Toolkit. Here's how its caching works:

Core Concepts
1. Cache Lifetime
Data is cached based on cache keys (the endpoint name + arguments)
Cache entries remain for a configurable time after the last subscriber unsubmits
Default cache lifetime: 60 seconds after all subscribers remove
2. Cache Keys
Every query is identified by a unique cache key:


// Example: getUserById(5) creates cache key "getUserById(5)"
const { data } = useGetUserByIdQuery(5);
3. How Caching Works

Component Mounts → Query Fetches Data → Data Stored in Cache
     ↓
Component Unmounts → Cache Timer Starts (default 60s)
     ↓
Component Remounts → If cache valid → Return cached data (no refetch)
     ↓
Cache Expires → Data Removed → Next mount triggers fresh fetch
Cache Configuration Options
keepUnusedDataFor (Global Default)

// In API definition
const api = createApi({
  baseQuery: fetchBaseQuery({ baseUrl: '/api' }),
  keepUnusedDataFor: 300, // 5 minutes instead of default 60s
  endpoints: (builder) => ({
    // ...
  }),
});
keepUnusedDataFor (Per Endpoint)

const api = createApi({
  baseQuery: fetchBaseQuery({ baseUrl: '/api' }),
  endpoints: (builder) => ({
    getUsers: builder.query({
      query: () => '/users',
      keepUnusedDataFor: 3600, // 1 hour for this endpoint
    }),
    getPosts: builder.query({
      query: () => '/posts',
      keepUnusedDataFor: 30, // 30 seconds for this endpoint
    }),
  }),
});
Cache Invalidation with Tags
Providing Tags (from queries)

const api = createApi({
  baseQuery: fetchBaseQuery({ baseUrl: '/api' }),
  tagTypes: ['User', 'Post'],
  endpoints: (builder) => ({
    getUsers: builder.query({
      query: () => '/users',
      providesTags: ['User'], // This query provides 'User' tag
    }),
    getUserById: builder.query({
      query: (id) => `/users/${id}`,
      providesTags: (result, error, id) => [{ type: 'User', id }],
    }),
  }),
});
Invalidating Tags (from mutations)

const api = createApi({
  baseQuery: fetchBaseQuery({ baseUrl: '/api' }),
  tagTypes: ['User', 'Post'],
  endpoints: (builder) => ({
    updateUser: builder.mutation({
      query: (user) => ({
        url: `/users/${user.id}`,
        method: 'PUT',
        body: user,
      }),
      invalidatesTags: ['User'], // Invalidates all 'User' tagged queries
    }),
    addPost: builder.mutation({
      query: (post) => ({
        url: '/posts',
        method: 'POST',
        body: post,
      }),
      invalidatesTags: ['Post'],
    }),
  }),
});
Cache Behavior Scenarios
Scenario 1: Same Query, Multiple Components

// Component A
const { data } = useGetUsersQuery();

// Component B (mounted later)
const { data } = useGetUsersQuery(); // Uses same cache, no refetch
Scenario 2: Different Arguments = Different Cache

const { data: user1 } = useGetUserByIdQuery(1); // Cache key: "getUserById(1)"
const { data: user2 } = useGetUserByIdQuery(2); // Cache key: "getUserById(2)"
Scenario 3: Force Refetch

const { data, refetch } = useGetUsersQuery();

// Force refetch even if cache is valid
<button onClick={refetch}>Refresh</button>
